<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01EK">Warning <span data-tag="01EK">4.5.4.15</span>.</a> Let $f: A \rightarrow B$ be a morphism of simplicial sets. By virtue of Corollary <a data-tag="00ZQ" href="/tag/00ZQ">3.3.7.7</a>, the morphism $f$ is anodyne if and only if it is both a monomorphism and a weak homotopy equivalence. Beware that the analogous assertion for inner anodyne morphisms is false. If $f$ is inner anodyne, then it is both a monomorphism (Remark <a data-tag="007D" href="/tag/007D">1.5.6.5</a>) and a categorical equivalence (Corollary <a data-tag="01EJ" href="/tag/01EJ">4.5.4.14</a>). However, the converse fails: a monomorphism $A \hookrightarrow B$ which is a categorical equivalence need not be inner anodyne. For example, an inner anodyne morphism of simplicial sets is automatically bijective on vertices (Exercise <a data-tag="01C3" href="/tag/01C3">1.5.6.6</a>). However, there can be other obstructions as well: see Example <a data-tag="01HD" href="/tag/01HD">4.5.4.16</a>. </p>
